1
00:00:00,200 --> 00:00:04,899
An AI agent can extend its capabilities by using
tools.

2
00:00:05,019 --> 00:00:12,902
A tool might be a small function, such as a
calculator, or an API call to an external service.

3
00:00:13,022 --> 00:00:18,331
The model generates a function call that identifies
the tool and its arguments.

4
00:00:18,451 --> 00:00:23,099
The function then runs, and its result returns to the
model.

5
00:00:23,219 --> 00:00:31,743
Keep those responsibilities clear: selecting a tool,
executing its code, and composing the response are

6
00:00:31,863 --> 00:00:33,932
separate steps.

7
00:00:34,833 --> 00:00:40,832
The lesson groups tool use around tasks that need
interaction with external systems.

8
00:00:40,952 --> 00:00:45,371
Information retrieval can query an API or database.

9
00:00:45,491 --> 00:00:51,254
Code execution can support calculations, reports or
simulations.

10
00:00:51,374 --> 00:00:58,482
Workflow automation connects several operations, while
support agents can interact with customer

11
00:00:58,602 --> 00:01:01,392
systems or knowledge bases.

12
00:01:01,512 --> 00:01:06,650
Content tools can check, summarise or evaluate text.

13
00:01:06,770 --> 00:01:12,509
Begin with the task, then identify which tool
capability it requires.

14
00:01:13,400 --> 00:01:17,894
A working tool-use design needs more than a function
description.

15
00:01:18,014 --> 00:01:20,853
Schemas define what is available.

16
00:01:20,973 --> 00:01:25,407
Execution logic decides how and when a tool is
invoked.

17
00:01:25,527 --> 00:01:32,180
Message handling connects user inputs, model
responses, calls and results.

18
00:01:32,300 --> 00:01:35,644
Integration connects the actual tools.

19
00:01:35,764 --> 00:01:42,317
Error handling and validation deal with failures,
parameters and unexpected responses.

20
00:01:42,437 --> 00:01:47,971
State management preserves context and earlier
interactions across turns.

21
00:01:48,091 --> 00:01:54,799
These are six distinct responsibilities, even when a
framework provides several of them together.

22
00:01:55,700 --> 00:01:59,059
The source illustrates a time lookup tool.

23
00:01:59,179 --> 00:02:06,847
Its schema gives the name get current time, explains
its purpose, and describes a location parameter.

24
00:02:06,967 --> 00:02:10,306
That parameter is a string and is required.

25
00:02:10,426 --> 00:02:14,465
This description helps the model construct a request.

26
00:02:14,585 --> 00:02:17,479
It does not execute the lookup.

27
00:02:17,599 --> 00:02:24,447
Developers still need a model that supports function
calling, the function descriptions, and the

28
00:02:24,567 --> 00:02:28,221
implementation of every function they expose.

29
00:02:29,100 --> 00:02:31,484
Follow the complete loop.

30
00:02:31,604 --> 00:02:36,568
Send the user request and the available function
descriptions to the model.

31
00:02:36,688 --> 00:02:40,411
The model can return a function name and arguments.

32
00:02:40,531 --> 00:02:44,690
Your application executes the corresponding function.

33
00:02:44,810 --> 00:02:49,674
The result is sent back to the model, which uses it to
respond.

34
00:02:49,794 --> 00:02:55,938
In the source example, a call identifier links the
function output to its call.

35
00:02:56,058 --> 00:03:03,046
A returned tool call is an intermediate request to run
code; it is not yet the answer to the user.

36
00:03:03,933 --> 00:03:07,437
The lesson shows two ways to connect tools.

37
00:03:07,557 --> 00:03:15,471
With Microsoft Agent Framework, decorated Python
functions can be serialised into schemas, and the

38
00:03:15,591 --> 00:03:19,919
framework handles the exchange between the model and
the code.

39
00:03:20,039 --> 00:03:26,463
Tools may also come from a server using the Model
Context Protocol, or MCP.

40
00:03:26,583 --> 00:03:32,851
The framework reads the server’s tool schemas and
offers those tools alongside local ones.

41
00:03:32,971 --> 00:03:38,350
When a remote tool is used, the generated query is
sent to that server.

42
00:03:39,233 --> 00:03:44,457
The source’s managed-service example groups tools into
two categories.

43
00:03:44,577 --> 00:03:48,351
Knowledge tools include search and file retrieval.

44
00:03:48,471 --> 00:03:53,215
Action tools include function calling and code
interpretation.

45
00:03:53,335 --> 00:04:00,473
A toolset makes multiple tools available together,
while threads keep the history of a conversation.

46
00:04:00,593 --> 00:04:06,182
The service example also handles tool invocation and
responses on the server.

47
00:04:06,302 --> 00:04:13,840
Keep the toolset and the thread distinct: one
organises capabilities; the other retains messages.

48
00:04:14,733 --> 00:04:19,432
Tools can touch real systems, so their permissions
matter.

49
00:04:19,552 --> 00:04:26,610
The lesson highlights risks from model-generated SQL,
including injection and database tampering.

50
00:04:26,730 --> 00:04:34,028
It recommends restricting database access to read-only
operations and, for the database services

51
00:04:34,148 --> 00:04:38,307
it names, assigning a read-only select role.

52
00:04:38,427 --> 00:04:44,711
It also describes a secure environment and a read-only
analytical database or warehouse.

53
00:04:44,831 --> 00:04:51,620
The implementation decision is concrete: the
application’s database access should match the

54
00:04:51,740 --> 00:04:54,249
restricted reading task.

55
00:04:55,133 --> 00:04:59,802
Consider this original question based on the source’s
time lookup.

56
00:04:59,922 --> 00:05:03,311
A user asks for the current time in a city.

57
00:05:03,431 --> 00:05:08,745
The model returns the function name get current time
and the location arguments.

58
00:05:08,865 --> 00:05:12,374
Has the user’s question already been answered?

59
00:05:12,494 --> 00:05:17,227
Name the next two application steps before revealing
the solution.

60
00:05:23,100 --> 00:05:26,539
The tool call is not yet the final answer.

61
00:05:26,659 --> 00:05:31,818
First, execute the selected function with the supplied
location.

62
00:05:31,938 --> 00:05:39,356
Next, return its result to the model, preserving the
call identifier used in the source example.

63
00:05:39,476 --> 00:05:43,265
The model can then use that result to answer the user.

64
00:05:43,385 --> 00:05:52,378
Recall the larger design: describe tools clearly,
connect execution and messages, preserve state, and

65
00:05:52,498 --> 00:05:55,352
validate inputs and results.
